### What is a ScopedValue?

A scoped value, finalized as JEP 506 in Java 25, is an immutable value bound for the dynamic scope of a bounded operation. You declare it as a `static final` field and bind it with `ScopedValue.where(KEY, value).run(...)` or `call(...)`.

```java
package com.bank.context;

import java.lang.ScopedValue;

public class RequestContext {

    // Declare as static final
    public static final ScopedValue<String> CURRENT_USER =
            ScopedValue.newInstance();

    public static final ScopedValue<String> REQUEST_ID =
            ScopedValue.newInstance();
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.context.RequestContext;
import java.lang.ScopedValue;

public class TransferEndpoint {

    public void handleRequest(String userId, String requestId) {
        // Bind for the duration of handleTransfer
        ScopedValue
            .where(RequestContext.CURRENT_USER, userId)
            .where(RequestContext.REQUEST_ID, requestId)
            .run(() -> handleTransfer());
        // Bindings are automatically removed here
    }

    private void handleTransfer() {
        // Read the bound values anywhere in the call tree
        String user = RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.get();
        String reqId = RequestContext.REQUEST_ID.get();
        System.out.println("Transfer by " + user
                + " [" + reqId + "]");
    }
}
```

### Why is ThreadLocal problematic for virtual threads?

ThreadLocal has three problems that get worse with virtual threads. It is mutable, so any code can call `set()` and change the value, making data flow hard to reason about. It has an unbounded lifetime, so on a pooled platform thread a value you forget to remove lingers and leaks. And inheritance to child threads via `InheritableThreadLocal` copies the whole map, which is wasteful when you create millions of virtual threads.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ThreadLocalLeakDemo {

    private static final ThreadLocal<String> CURRENT_USER =
            new ThreadLocal<>();

    public void handleRequest(String userId) {
        CURRENT_USER.set(userId); // Mutable: any code can change
        try {
            processTransfer();
        } finally {
            CURRENT_USER.remove(); // Easy to forget!
        }
    }

    // If remove() is not called, the value leaks
    // On a pooled thread, the next request sees the old user
    // With millions of virtual threads, InheritableThreadLocal
    // copies the map for each one

    private void processTransfer() {
        String user = CURRENT_USER.get();
        System.out.println("Transfer by " + user);
    }
}
```

### How is ScopedValue different from ThreadLocal? (Comparison)

| Feature | ScopedValue | ThreadLocal |
|---|---|---|
| Mutability | Immutable (no `set()`) | Mutable (`set()` anytime) |
| Lifetime | Bounded by `run()`/`call()` scope | Unbounded until `remove()` |
| Leak risk | None, automatic teardown | High if `remove()` forgotten |
| Child thread inheritance | Cheap, no map copy | InheritableThreadLocal copies map |
| Data flow | Obvious, read-only | Hard to trace, any code can mutate |
| Virtual thread cost | Lightweight | Expensive map per thread |

### How does nested rebinding work?

If a sub-operation needs a different value, you rebind in a nested scope. The new binding applies only inside the nested scope and reverts when it ends.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.context.RequestContext;
import java.lang.ScopedValue;

public class OperatorService {

    // Rajesh (operator) acts on behalf of Priya (customer)
    public void actOnBehalf(String operatorId, String customerId) {
        ScopedValue
            .where(RequestContext.CURRENT_USER, operatorId)
            .run(() -> {
                System.out.println("Operator: "
                    + RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.get()); // Rajesh

                // Nested rebind for sub-operation
                ScopedValue
                    .where(RequestContext.CURRENT_USER, customerId)
                    .run(() -> {
                        System.out.println("Acting as: "
                            + RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.get()); // Priya
                        processCustomerTransfer();
                    });

                // Reverts to Rajesh after nested scope
                System.out.println("Back to: "
                    + RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.get()); // Rajesh
            });
    }

    private void processCustomerTransfer() {
        System.out.println("Processing for "
            + RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.get());
    }
}
```

```text
Operator: Rajesh
Acting as: Priya
Processing for Priya
Back to: Rajesh
```

### How do scoped values work with structured concurrency?

Scoped values are designed to work with `StructuredTaskScope`. When a scope forks child tasks, they transparently and cheaply inherit the enclosing scoped-value bindings without copying a map per thread.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.context.RequestContext;
import java.lang.ScopedValue;
import java.util.concurrent.StructuredTaskScope;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferApprovalService {

    public boolean approveTransfer(String userId) throws Exception {
        return ScopedValue
            .where(RequestContext.CURRENT_USER, userId)
            .call(() -> runChecks());
    }

    private boolean runChecks() throws Exception {
        try (var scope = new StructuredTaskScope
                .ShutdownOnFailure()) {

            // Both child tasks inherit CURRENT_USER cheaply
            var creditTask = scope.fork(() -> {
                String user = RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.get();
                return checkCredit(user);
            });

            var fraudTask = scope.fork(() -> {
                String user = RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.get();
                return checkFraud(user);
            });

            scope.join().throwIfFailed();
            return creditTask.get() && fraudTask.get();
        }
    }

    private boolean checkCredit(String user) {
        System.out.println("Credit check for " + user);
        return true;
    }

    private boolean checkFraud(String user) {
        System.out.println("Fraud check for " + user);
        return true;
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes with ScopedValue?

The first mistake is trying to call `get()` outside a bound scope, which throws `NoSuchElementException`. The second is using scoped values for mutable state or accumulators, which they are not designed for.

```java
// WRONG: reading outside a bound scope
String user = RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.get();
// Throws NoSuchElementException if not bound!

// CORRECT: check before reading
if (RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.isBound()) {
    String user = RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.get();
}

// WRONG: trying to use ScopedValue as mutable state
// ScopedValue has no set() method, so this is impossible
// Use a different mechanism for accumulators
```

### When should you use ScopedValue vs ThreadLocal?

Use `ScopedValue` for immutable context shared implicitly down a call tree, such as the current user, a request ID, a tenant ID, or a trace context. Use `ThreadLocal` for mutable per-thread caches or accumulators where the value changes during processing.

### What about the call() variant?

When the bounded operation returns a value or throws a checked exception, use `call()` instead of `run()`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.context.RequestContext;
import java.lang.ScopedValue;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BalanceService {

    public BigDecimal getBalance(String userId, String accountId)
            throws Exception {
        return ScopedValue
            .where(RequestContext.CURRENT_USER, userId)
            .call(() -> loadBalance(accountId));
    }

    private BigDecimal loadBalance(String accountId) {
        String user = RequestContext.CURRENT_USER.get();
        System.out.println("Loading balance for " + user
                + " account " + accountId);
        return new BigDecimal("150000.00");
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Scoped values are specifically for immutable context shared implicitly down a call tree. The immutability and automatic teardown are the entire point, because they make the context tamper-proof and leak-proof. The lightweight inheritance is what keeps them fast at virtual-thread scale where InheritableThreadLocal would be too expensive. The newest API iterations fold well with StructuredTaskScope, giving context propagation for free.
